=3D"FAQReadMore_questionSection__bgir5"><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_question_=
_MfGL5 "><p>1. What is the principle of homogeneity, and why is it importan=
t for dimensional analysis?</p></div><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_changingSign=
__jc_sp"><span class=3D"FAQReadMore_arrowUp__lckY5"></span></div></div><div=
 class=3D"FAQReadMore_answerSection__kCxEr "><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_top_=
_5yhJu"><p>The <strong>principle of homogeneity</strong> states that an equ=
ation is dimensionally correct only if the dimensions of all the terms on b=
oth sides of the equation are the same. This principle is the foundation of=
 dimensional analysis because it allows us to check the correctness of a ph=
ysical equation, as we can only add or subtract quantities that have the sa=
me physical dimensions.</p></div><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_top__5yhJu"></di=
v></div></div><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_questionCard__ywihi   "><div class=
=3D"FAQReadMore_questionSection__bgir5"><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_question_=
_MfGL5 "><p>2. What are the seven fundamental quantities in physics and the=
ir dimensional symbols?</p></div><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_changingSign__jc=
_sp"><span class=3D"FAQReadMore_arrowUp__lckY5"></span></div></div><div cla=
ss=3D"FAQReadMore_answerSection__kCxEr "><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_top__5yh=
Ju"><p>For your exams, it's crucial to know the seven fundamental quantitie=
s and their dimensional symbols as per the SI system. They are:</p><ul><li>=
Length (L)</li><li>Mass (M)</li><li>Time (T)</li><li>Electric Current (A)</=
li><li>Thermodynamic Temperature (K)</li><li>Amount of Substance (mol)</li>=
<li>Luminous Intensity (cd)</li></ul></div><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_top__5=
yhJu"></div></div></div><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_questionCard__ywihi  FAQR=
eadMore_active__zMbHK "><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_questionSection__bgir5"><=
div class=3D"FAQReadMore_question__MfGL5 "><p>3. How can I use dimensional =
analysis to check if a physics formula is correct?</p></div><div class=3D"F=
AQReadMore_changingSign__jc_sp"><span class=3D"FAQReadMore_arrowDown__eRTxB=
"></span></div></div><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_answerSection__kCxEr "><div =
class=3D"FAQReadMore_top__5yhJu"><p>To check a formula, you need to apply t=
he principle of homogeneity. First, write down the dimensional formula for =
the quantity on the left-hand side (LHS) of the equation. Then, write the d=
imensional formulas for all the quantities on the right-hand side (RHS). Si=
mplify the dimensions on the RHS. If the final dimensions on the RHS match =
the dimensions on the LHS, the formula is <strong>dimensionally correct</st=
rong>.</p></div><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_top__5yhJu"></div></div></div><di=
v class=3D"FAQReadMore_questionCard__ywihi   "><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_qu=
estionSection__bgir5"><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_question__MfGL5 "><p>4. Wha=
t types of questions on dimensional analysis are frequently asked in Class =
11 exams?</p></div><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_changingSign__jc_sp"><span cla=
ss=3D"FAQReadMore_arrowUp__lckY5"></span></div></div><div class=3D"FAQReadM=
ore_answerSection__kCxEr "><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_top__5yhJu"><p>For the=
 CBSE 2025-26 session, you can expect questions that test three main skills=
: 1) Checking the dimensional consistency of equations, 2) Deriving the rel=
ationship between different physical quantities, and 3) Converting a physic=
al quantity from one system of units to another. You may also be asked 1-ma=
rk questions on the dimensions of physical constants or the limitations of =
this method.</p></div><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_top__5yhJu"></div></div></d=
iv><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_questionCard__ywihi   "><div class=3D"FAQReadM=
ore_questionSection__bgir5"><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_question__MfGL5 "><p>=
5. If an equation is dimensionally correct, does it guarantee that the equa=
tion is physically correct? Why or why not?</p></div><div class=3D"FAQReadM=
ore_changingSign__jc_sp"><span class=3D"FAQReadMore_arrowUp__lckY5"></span>=
</div></div><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_answerSection__kCxEr "><div class=3D"=
FAQReadMore_top__5yhJu"><p>No, a dimensionally correct equation is not nece=
ssarily a physically correct one. Dimensional analysis cannot determine the=
 value of <strong>dimensionless constants</strong> (like 1/2, =CF=80, or 2)=
. For example, the formulas for kinetic energy, K =3D =C2=BD mv=C2=B2 and K=
 =3D mv=C2=B2, are both dimensionally correct, but only the first one is ph=
ysically right. Therefore, dimensional correctness is a necessary but not s=
ufficient condition for an equation to be physically correct.</p></div><div=
 class=3D"FAQReadMore_top__5yhJu"></div></div></div><div class=3D"FAQReadMo=
re_questionCard__ywihi   "><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_questionSection__bgir5=
"><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_question__MfGL5 "><p>6. What are the key limita=
tions of dimensional analysis that I should know for my exam?</p></div><div=
 class=3D"FAQReadMore_changingSign__jc_sp"><span class=3D"FAQReadMore_arrow=
Up__lckY5"></span></div></div><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_answerSection__kCxE=
r "><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_top__5yhJu"><p>Dimensional analysis has sever=
al important limitations that are often asked in exams. Key points to remem=
ber are:</p><ul><li>It cannot determine the value of dimensionless constant=
s.</li><li>It cannot be used for equations involving trigonometric, logarit=
hmic, or exponential functions.</li><li>It fails if a physical quantity dep=
ends on more than three other fundamental quantities.</li><li>It cannot be =
used to derive a formula that contains the sum or difference of two or more=
 quantities.</li></ul><p></p></div><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_top__5yhJu"></=
div></div></div><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_questionCard__ywihi   "><div clas=
s=3D"FAQReadMore_questionSection__bgir5"><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_question=
__MfGL5 "><p>7. Besides checking formulas, what is another major applicatio=
n of dimensional analysis that is important for 3-mark or 5-mark questions?=
</p></div><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_changingSign__jc_sp"><span class=3D"FAQ=
ReadMore_arrowUp__lckY5"></span></div></div><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_answe=
rSection__kCxEr "><div class=3D"FAQReadMore_top__5yhJu"><p>A very important=
 application is to <strong>derive the relationship</strong> between differe=
nt physical quantities. If you know that a certain quantity depends on seve=
ral other factors, you can use their dimensions to find the exact mathemati=
cal expression that connects them. For example, you can derive the formula =
for the time period of a simple pendulum by knowing it depends on its lengt=
h (l) and acceleration due to gravity (g).</p></div><div class=3D"FAQReadMo=
re_top__5yhJu"></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class=
